Q: Vite build HTML file snippet with script tag inside, can the JS be transpiled? A: <p>The issue is that Vite treats HTML files as <strong>static assets</strong> and doesn't apply transpilation rules to them. JavaScript inside &lt;script&gt;tags remains untransformed. Here are a few solutions:</p>
<p><em><strong>Solution 1: Create a Vite Plugin</strong></em></p>
<p>The proper way is to write a plugin that processes HTML components:</p>
<pre><code>// vite.config.js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import sassGlobImports from 'vite-plugin-sass-glob-import';
import fs from 'fs';
import { transformAsync } from '@babel/core';

const processHTMLComponents = {
  name: 'process-html-components',
  async load(id) {
    if (id.endsWith('.html') &amp;&amp; id.includes('components')) {
      let html = fs.readFileSync(id, 'utf-8');

      // Transpile inline JavaScript
      html = await transformInlineScripts(html);

      return `export default ${JSON.stringify(html)};`;
    }
  }
};

async function transformInlineScripts(html) {
  const scriptRegex = /&lt;script[^&gt;]*&gt;([\s\S]*?)&lt;\/script&gt;/g;
  let match;

  while ((match = scriptRegex.exec(html)) !== null) {
    const code = match[1];
    const result = await transformAsync(code, {
      presets: [['@babel/preset-env', { targets: 'es2015' }]]
    });
    html = html.replace(match[0], `&lt;script&gt;${result.code}&lt;/script&gt;`);
  }
  return html;
}

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [sassGlobImports(), processHTMLComponents],
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      input: {
        main: './src/js/app',
        editor: './src/scss/editor.scss',
        accordion: 'components/accordion.html',
        calendar: 'components/calendar.html'
      },
      output: {
        assetFileNames: () =&gt; '[name][extname]',
        chunkFileNames: 'main.min.js',
        entryFileNames: 'main.min.js',
      },
    },
    target: 'es2015',
    outDir: 'app/build/',
  },
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Install Babel:</strong></p>
<pre><code>npm install --save-dev @babel/core @babel/preset-env
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><em><strong>Solution 2: Node.js Script for Pre-processing</strong></em></p>
<p>If the plugin seems too complex, create a script to process components before the build:</p>
<pre><code>// scripts/build-components.js
import fs from 'fs';
import path from 'path';
import { transformAsync } from '@babel/core';

const componentsDir = './components';
const outputDir = './app/build/components';

async function buildComponents() {
  if (!fs.existsSync(outputDir)) {
    fs.mkdirSync(outputDir, { recursive: true });
  }

  const files = fs.readdirSync(componentsDir).filter(f =&gt; f.endsWith('.html'));

  for (const file of files) {
    let html = fs.readFileSync(path.join(componentsDir, file), 'utf-8');

    const scriptRegex = /&lt;script[^&gt;]*&gt;([\s\S]*?)&lt;\/script&gt;/g;
    let match;

    while ((match = scriptRegex.exec(html)) !== null) {
      const result = await transformAsync(match[1], {
        presets: [['@babel/preset-env', { targets: 'es2015' }]]
      });
      html = html.replace(match[0], `&lt;script&gt;${result.code}&lt;/script&gt;`);
    }

    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(outputDir, file), html);
    console.log(`✓ ${file}`);
  }
}

buildComponents();
</code></pre>
<p><strong>In package.json:</strong></p>
<p>json</p>
<pre><code>&quot;scripts&quot;: {
  &quot;build&quot;: &quot;node scripts/build-components.js &amp;&amp; vite build&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3><em><strong>Solution 3: Just Use ES5 Syntax</strong></em></h3>
<p>If you don't want to complicate things, write JavaScript in the older format:</p>
<p>html</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;calendar&quot;&gt;
    &lt;script&gt;
    var wrapper = document.querySelector('.calendar');
    var trigger = wrapper.querySelector('input');
    console.log(trigger);
    &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Fine grained reactivity for derived nested dictionary A: <p>Ok the issue is that you should not do:</p>
<pre><code>return $derived…
</code></pre>
<p>but instead</p>
<pre><code>const foo = $derived
return () =&gt; foo
</code></pre>
<p>otherwise the value would be returned and not its reactive part. I came up with some solutions like this one:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script lang=&quot;ts&quot;&gt;
    let l : number[] = $state([])
    const myExpensiveFunction = (acc: number, lx: number, i: number, verbose : boolean = true) =&gt; {
        if (verbose) {
      console.log(`Running my expensive function on acc ${acc} with l[i] = ${lx} and i=${i}`);
    }
        return acc + lx
    }
  class MapReduce&lt;A, B&gt; {
    // Contained the list of derived value for m
    m: (() =&gt; B)[] = []
    l: A[]
    f: (acc: B, x:A, index: number) =&gt; B
    initialValue: B
    length: number
    constructor (l: A[], f: (acc: B, x:A, index: number) =&gt; B, initialValue: B) {
      this.l = l
      this.f = f
      this.initialValue = $derived(initialValue)
      this.length = $derived(this.l.length + 1);
    }

    get(i: number) : B {
      if (this.m?.[i] === undefined) { // This index was never queried before: we
        if (i === 0) {
          // We must return a function (=closure) otherwise a copy of the value will be sent instead
          this.m[i] = () =&gt; this.initialValue
        }
        else {
          const foo = $derived(this.f(this.get(i - 1), this.l[i-1], i-1))
          this.m[i] = () =&gt; foo
        }
      }
      return this.m[i]()
    }

    *[Symbol.iterator](){
            for(let i = 0; i &lt; this.length; i += 1){
                yield this.get(i)
            }
        }
  }
  let m = new MapReduce(l, myExpensiveFunction, 0)

  // To check the result
  let mInneficient = $derived(l.reduce((acc, x, i) =&gt; {
        acc.push(myExpensiveFunction(acc[acc.length - 1], x, i, false));
        return acc
    }, [0]));
&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;First list l&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can edit me here (just click on the item to change its value):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
  &lt;button onclick={() =&gt; l.splice(0, 0, 1)} class=&quot;text-sm bg-blue-100 border rounded-lg px-2 py-0.5&quot;&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;
    {#each l as x, i}
    &lt;span class=&quot;inline-div m-3 p-2 rounded-lg&quot;&gt;
          &lt;span contenteditable role=&quot;button&quot; tabindex=&quot;0&quot; onblur={(e) =&gt; l[i] = parseInt((e.target as HTMLElement).innerText)} onkeydown={(e) =&gt; {if (e.key === 'Enter') {(e.target as HTMLElement).blur()}}}&gt;{x}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;button onclick={() =&gt; l.splice(i, 1)} class=&quot;text-sm bg-red-100 border rounded-lg px-2 py-0.5&quot;&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button onclick={() =&gt; l.splice(i+1, 0, 1)} class=&quot;text-sm bg-blue-100 border rounded-lg px-2 py-0.5&quot;&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;
    {/each}
&lt;/p&gt;

Summary (just to check it changed):
&lt;p&gt;
    {#each l as x, i}
        {x},
    {/each}
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;Second derived list m&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    {#each m as x, i}
        &lt;span&gt;{x}, &lt;/span&gt;
    {/each}
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Correct (but inneficient) solution:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    {#each mInneficient as x, i}
        {x},
    {/each}
&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You can try it here <a href="https://svelte.dev/playground/a3def9eb8ce34ece88af90bd9351f470?version=latest" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://svelte.dev/playground/a3def9eb8ce34ece88af90bd9351f470?version=latest</a></p>
<p>See also:</p>
<ul>
<li>with a Proxy <a href="https://svelte.dev/playground/e0905403d8b64886a3c8ca50731dc75e?version=latest" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://svelte.dev/playground/e0905403d8b64886a3c8ca50731dc75e?version=latest</a></li>
<li>see also other solutions in <a href="https://discord.com/channels/457912077277855764/1505869515249025077" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://discord.com/channels/457912077277855764/1505869515249025077</a> notably one solution that keeps the list l and a cache of it, and when it changes it compares the new value of l with its cache to see what changed, and manually recompute what is necessary (I find it a bit less elegant than the recursive solution posted here).</li>
</ul>

Q: How do I make sure 2 instances of my class have the same hash code? A: <pre><code>using System;
using System.Text;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello World");
        HashSet&lt;Position&gt; hash = new HashSet&lt;Position&gt;();
        Position position = new Position(3, 1);
        Position position2 = new Position(3, 1);
        hash.Add(position);
        Console.WriteLine(hash.Contains(position2));
    }
}


public class Position
 {
     public int row, col;

     public Position(int row, int col) {
       this.row =  row;
        this.col =  col;
     }

     public override bool Equals(object obj)
     {
         return ((Position)obj).row == row &amp;&amp; ((Position)obj).col == col;
     }

     public override int GetHashCode()
     {
        Console.WriteLine((row + col) * (row - col + 1));
        return (row + col) * (row - col + 1);
     }
 }
</code></pre>
